Changes of this fork
====================

This file lists what the 'storage' branch of this fork adds to SnapRAID Daemon
by Andrea Mazzoleni (https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon).

The original project is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later, and so are the
additions. Files that already existed keep the copyright of their author.
The new files (daemon/storage.c, daemon/storage.h, daemon/arrays.c,
daemon/arrays.h, js/storage.js and js/arrays.js of both UIs, test/ui/,
installer.sh) are copyright of Robert Paleka.

The additions were written with the help of an AI coding assistant (Claude).
The AGENTS.md file of the original project asks agents to never commit to
git. That rule is followed for the original repository: nothing was committed
or proposed there. On this fork the commits were made on request of the fork
owner.


What is added
-------------

Everything is disabled by default. It is enabled by the new 'net_storage_admin'
option, and only if the API is bound to the loopback address, or it is
protected by 'net_auth_credential' or 'net_acl'. Linux only.

* Storage page in the Commander and Brutale UIs, with a diagram of the array
  and of the pool, an advisor, and a guided setup.

* Inventory of the disks, partitions, filesystems and mount points.

* Editor of the SnapRAID configuration file, with validation.

* Format (ext4, xfs) and mount of disks, with /etc/fstab kept up to date.
  A backup of the previous fstab is kept as /etc/fstab.snapraidd.bak.

* mergerfs pools: create, change and remove, with branches and policies.

* Balance of the files among the data disks, search of duplicate files in
  the branches of a pool, and a file browser.

* Recovery of failed disks, also with the array in a degraded state: fix of
  the selected disks, check, and sync, in one command.

* Arrays up to the limits of SnapRAID: six parity levels, split parity.

* More arrays in the same daemon. The array of the main configuration file
  is the "default" one; every other array has a file of its own in the arrays
  directory (/etc/snapraidd.d/NAME.conf) with its SnapRAID configuration,
  schedule, thresholds, hooks and notifications, and its own tasks and logs.
  Arrays are created and removed from the UI, from the REST API, or putting
  and removing the file and reloading the daemon. An installation with one
  array works as before, with the same files and the same API.

* One array at a time runs the commands working on the whole disks, unless
  'array_parallel' is set: then arrays that share no physical disk run
  together. Commands that only look at the disks are never delayed.

* A disk belongs to one array: a SnapRAID configuration using a data disk,
  a parity disk or a content file of another array is refused.

* More pools, and filesystems that are in no array. The inventory tells for
  every mount and every branch of a pool which array protects it, or that
  nothing does.

* Arrays page in both UIs: the health of every array, a map of arrays, pools
  and separate mounts, and the move of a data disk with its files to another
  array. A switcher in the sidebar chooses the array the other pages work on.

* A balance and a sync of the same disks keep away from each other: the
  balance is refused while an array of the pool is working, and the array
  waits for a running balance.

* What is saved in /etc/fstab never stops the boot: filesystems are saved by
  UUID and with 'nofail', pools with 'nofail' through the helper of FUSE.

* A policy that mergerfs doesn't know is refused before it reaches the pool.

* Reports, notification subjects and hooks (SNAPRAID_ARRAY) name the array.

* A machine where SnapRAID is already used from the command line: the
  installer converts a content file of SnapRAID 13 or older before upgrading
  the engine, stops without changing anything when that takes a real sync or
  the engine was built from the sources, and starts the daemon without a
  schedule and without spin down. The Storage page tells a content file in
  the old format, and a daemon without a schedule. See "An array already in
  use" in the README.

* A sync made from the command line while the daemon runs reaches it at the
  next periodic probe: the content file is read again.

* The SnapRAID configuration replaced from the UI is kept as
  snapraid.conf.snapraidd.bak, and no more as .bak, a name people use for
  their own copy.

* Tests: 'make check' also runs a selftest of the storage code and, if
  Node.js is installed, the tests of the logic of the Storage and Arrays
  pages.


